WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.080
They took an offer they couldn’t&nbsp;
refuse and changed cinema forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.800
From cryin’ crooners to beloved bodyguards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.960
these Godfather icons may no longer be&nbsp;
in the family, but their memory lives on.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.960
John Cazale's short-lived but legendary film&nbsp;
career didn't really take off until 1972,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.240
when Francis Ford Coppola cast him as Fredo&nbsp;
Corleone in "The Godfather." As the doomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.680
middle son of the Corleone family, Cazale's&nbsp;
heartbreaking performance soon became iconic.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.160
"I'm smart! And I want respect!"

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.920
After betraying the family in "The&nbsp;
Godfather: Part II," Fredo was tragically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.680
assassinated on the order of his younger&nbsp;
brother, Michael, played by Al Pacino.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:43.387
"I know it was you, Fredo. You broke my heart."

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.160
Perhaps the most interesting thing about Cazale's&nbsp;
films apart from "The Godfather" and its first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.360
sequel is that each of the three projects had a&nbsp;
major connection to the "The Godfather" films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.080
1974's "The Conversation" was also&nbsp;
directed by Coppola, while the 1975&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.600
crime drama "Dog Day Afternoon"&nbsp;
reunited Cazale with Pacino.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.120
In his final screen role,&nbsp;
1978's Best Picture Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.160
winner "The Deer Hunter," Cazale&nbsp;
starred opposite Robert De Niro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.320
who portrayed the young Vito Corleone so&nbsp;
memorably in "The Godfather: Part II."

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.480
Tragically, Cazale's eleven-year battle&nbsp;
with lung cancer ended on March 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.320
1978, with his girlfriend "Deer Hunter" co-star&nbsp;
Meryl Streep, by his side. Aware of his terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.800
illness during the production, director Michael&nbsp;
Cimino reportedly rearranged the shooting order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.920
of the film so Cazale could complete his scenes.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Cazale never got the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.440
to celebrate the release of "The Deer Hunter." The&nbsp;
film premiered in Los Angeles in December of 1978,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.200
nine months after his tragically&nbsp;
premature death at the age of 42.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.080
Unlike many of the bodyguards who protected the&nbsp;
Corleone family, Richard Bright's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.800
Al Neri, survived all three "Godfather" films.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.000
"The old dons were pretty much wiped out. The&nbsp;
ones that survived made a deal with Joey Zaza."

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.920
Bright had a prolific career that&nbsp;
goes all the way back to 1957,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.320
when he first began appearing in films,&nbsp;
television shows, and stage plays. Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.560
in 1971 he played Al Pacino's character's brother,&nbsp;
Hank, in "The Panic in Needle Park" and in 1972,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.640
played "The Thief" alongside Steve&nbsp;
Mcqueen and Ali McGraw in "The Getaway."

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.080
Apart from playing Neri in all three "Godfather"&nbsp;
films, the steely-eyed actor alternated between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.480
bad guy and good guy roles in a variety of films,&nbsp;
including "Marathon Man," "Red Heat," and "Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.320
Upon a Time in America." He also appeared&nbsp;
in a string of television series, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.080
the prison drama "Oz," the iconic mafia series&nbsp;
"The Sopranos," and the "Law &amp; Order" franchise.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.320
Bright died in a tragic accident at the age of&nbsp;
68. On February 18, 2006, while crossing a street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.200
on New York's Upper West Side, he was hit and&nbsp;
killed by a bus. Fourteen years after his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.120
Bright's contribution to "The Godfather"&nbsp;
saga was honored once more with Coppola's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.480
redux of the third "Godfather" film, a re-edited&nbsp;
version retitled "The Godfather, Coda: The Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.600
of Michael Corleone." The third chapter features&nbsp;
more of Al Neri than any other "Godfather" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.827
as Neri has escalated in the ranks to become a&nbsp;
close confidant of Michael and Connie Corleone.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.400
Singer-turned-actor Al Martino played Johnny&nbsp;
Fontane, the silky-voiced crooner looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.334
for his big break in motion pictures in&nbsp;
the first chapter of "The Godfather."

00:03:14.504 --> 00:03:18.880
"Johnny, Johnny, cantare&nbsp;
una canzone, sing a song!"

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.600
In one memorable early scene, while pleading his&nbsp;
case to Marlon Brando's Vito Corleone, crying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.600
about how he missed out on a role in a film, the&nbsp;
weepy Fontane is slapped by the incensed Don Vito.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.200
"You can act like a man! Whatsa matter with you?"

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.880
And, spoiler alert — Johnny got the role after a&nbsp;
studio executive was forced to accept an offer he,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.680
well… really couldn't refuse! In real life,&nbsp;
Martino stuck to his successful career as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.080
singer, scoring a slew of hits over the years.&nbsp;
While he's most well-known for the song "Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.280
Eyes," other hits include: "Here in My Heart," and&nbsp;
"I Love You Because." His songs also appeared on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.560
movie soundtracks, most notably as the title track&nbsp;
of the eerie Bette Davis thriller "Hush...Hush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.160
Sweet Charlotte." Naturally, Martino's&nbsp;
songs were featured on the soundtracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.560
of "The Godfather" and "The Godfather: Part&nbsp;
III," where he reprised the role of Fontane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.480
Martino was 82 years old when he died of a heart&nbsp;
attack in Pennsylvania on October 13, 2009.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.280
While some actors only appeared in a scene or&nbsp;
two in "The Godfather" trilogy, they still become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.000
etched in the minds of movie fans. Perhaps the&nbsp;
most memorable of these supporting players?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.400
John Marley, who played defiant studio&nbsp;
executive Jack Woltz in the first film.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.360
Woltz appeared in two notable scenes in the film,&nbsp;
where Vito Corleone's adopted son, Tom Hagen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.160
played by Robert Duvall, arrived in Hollywood&nbsp;
with an offer the studio head couldn't refuse:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.960
cast Johnny Fontane in his new film…&nbsp;
or else. Woltz blasted Hagen's offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.840
only to wake up the next morning&nbsp;
with the bloodied head of his prize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.080
stallion tucked into bed with him. Not&nbsp;
surprisingly Fontane landed the role.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.720
Marley was a prolific actor, having amassed&nbsp;
more than 170 film and television credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.040
over his 35-year career. His appearance&nbsp;
as Ali McGraw's father in "Love Story"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.720
was a particular highlight, earning him&nbsp;
a Best Supporting Actor Oscar nomination.

00:05:01.441 --> 00:05:06.241
"Now, don't talk like that Oliver. A father's&nbsp;
love is something to cherish and respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.680
It's a rare thing."

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.240
Marley had roles in several television series,&nbsp;
including "Hawaii Five-0" and "The Twilight Zone."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.240
The actor died after open heart surgery&nbsp;
on May 22, 1984. He was 76 years old.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.080
Like John Marley's Jack Woltz, the appearance&nbsp;
of Lenny Montana's Luca Brasi is brief, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.200
memorable. Brasi, Don Vito Corleone's deadliest&nbsp;
assassin, first appears in the film rehearsing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.320
his speech and then presenting a gift to the Don&nbsp;
for his daughter, Connie, on her wedding day.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.440
"Who is he, what's his name?"

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.800
"His name is Luca Brasi. He&nbsp;
helps my father out sometimes."

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.200
Later, Brasi is stabbed and garroted in a&nbsp;
vicious scene on the orders of Corleone foe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.920
Virgil Sollozzo, played by Al Lettieri.&nbsp;
After Brasi's death, Sollozzo delivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.160
a message to the Corleone family that their&nbsp;
fearsome henchman "sleeps with the fishes."

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.560
"The Godfather" was actually Montana's first&nbsp;
credited film role. Born Lenny Passaforo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.720
he made his living as a professional wrestler,&nbsp;
going under the name Lenny "The Bull" Montana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.600
in the 1950s and '60s. Keeping the Montana&nbsp;
moniker for his film career, the 6-foot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.560
6-inch actor's powerful physique not only came in&nbsp;
handy for landing his role in "The Godfather," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.960
other films that required a heavy, acting with&nbsp;
Harvey Keitel in "Fingers," and Jackie Chan in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:23.668
"Battle Creek Brawl." Montana died of a heart&nbsp;
attack on May 12, 1992. He was 66 years old.

00:06:23.668 --> 00:06:27.360
Abe Vigoda became best known&nbsp;
as Detective Phil Fish on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.520
classic cop sitcom "Barney Miller" and&nbsp;
later on his own spinoff series, "Fish."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.520
But the actor first made a splash a&nbsp;
few years earlier as Salvatore Tessio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.080
a high-ranking member of the Corleone family&nbsp;
who delivered an iconic line in "The Godfather."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.960
During a meeting with Michael Corleone, Sonny&nbsp;
Corleone, and Tom Hagen, Tessio enters the room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.000
with a bullet proof vest wrapped in brown paper.&nbsp;
The package contains, famously, a dead fish.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.400
"That's a Sicilian message. It means&nbsp;
Luca Brassi sleeps with the fishes."

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.254
When Tessio betrays the family and finds himself&nbsp;
facing a similar fate, he begs for mercy:

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.834
"Tom, can you get me off the&nbsp;
hook? For old times' sake?"

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.960
Like many of his fellow "Godfather"&nbsp;
actors, Vigoda had a prolific career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.200
with nearly 100 roles to his name. They&nbsp;
included early television appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.800
on such series as "Barney Miller,"&nbsp;
"Dark Shadows," and "The Love Boat."

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.440
Vigoda's film work included "Joe Versus the&nbsp;
Volcano" and "Good Burger." He was also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.320
favorite and frequent guest on "Late Night With&nbsp;
Conan O'Brien." Vigoda, who outlasted persistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.800
rumors of his death, passed away from natural&nbsp;
causes on January 26, 2016. He was 94 years old.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.960
Like many of his castmates, Richard&nbsp;
S. Castellano rose to prominence after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.080
appearing in the first "Godfather." Before being&nbsp;
cast as Corleone family captain Peter Clemenza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.200
Castellano had appeared in about a dozen film&nbsp;
and television productions. Clemenza became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.427
famous for uttering these six classic words,&nbsp;
which Castellano says he partly improvised:

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.614
"Leave the gun. Take the cannoli."

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.200
Castellano, who only appeared in the first&nbsp;
"Godfather" film, worked steadily afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.440
mostly in television. His credits included&nbsp;
the lead role in the sitcom "The Super,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.080
where he played a building superintendent. He&nbsp;
took another lead as Joe Vitale in the sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.120
"Joe and Sons," and appeared as a crime heavy once&nbsp;
more in the mini-series "The Gangster Chronicles."

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.747
Castellano died on December 10, 1988,&nbsp;
after suffering a heart attack. He was 55.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.960
While Bruno Kirby is predominantly known&nbsp;
for his comedic work in such films as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.280
"When Harry Met Sally," "City Slickers,"&nbsp;
and "This is Spinal Tap”, his early work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.920
also included a dramatic turn as the young&nbsp;
Peter Clemenza in "The Godfather Part II."

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.800
Francis Ford Coppola and Mario Puzo took the&nbsp;
first "Godfather" sequel in a different direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.040
as the film's narrative hopped back and forth&nbsp;
between the past and the present to recount the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.920
rise of Robert DeNiro's young Vito Corleone before&nbsp;
he became a don in New York's criminal underworld.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.760
Kirby's career was well underway before he&nbsp;
portrayed the young Clemenza. His credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.400
included the short-lived sitcom "The Super," where&nbsp;
he played one of Richard S. Castellano's sons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.400
Kirby would go on to claim an impressive&nbsp;
70 screen credits in his 35-year career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.280
including roles opposite Marlon Brando and Matthew&nbsp;
Broderick in the mob comedy "The Freshman."

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.440
"Tragically, I suffer from a gambling problem."

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.160
Kirby played opposite another "Godfather"&nbsp;
alum, Al Pacino, in "Donnie Brasco." He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.560
worked with Garry Shandling on both "It's Garry&nbsp;
Shandling's Show" and "The Larry Sanders Show."

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.320
Kirby's life and career were&nbsp;
cut short on August 14, 2006,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.560
when he passed away due to complications&nbsp;
from leukemia at the age of 57.

00:09:26.701 --> 00:09:30.640
Michael V. Gazzo's Frank Pentangeli&nbsp;
was a major figure in "The Godfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:35.040
Part II," as the Corleone family caporegime&nbsp;
who replaced Peter Clemenza and ran the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.401
family's operations in New York City while&nbsp;
Michael Corleone relocated to Las Vegas.

00:09:39.586 --> 00:09:43.200
"Hey, what's with the food around&nbsp;
here? Kid comes up to me in a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.120
jacket gives me a Ritz cracker with&nbsp;
chopped liver and he says canapes."

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.320
Eventually, in an effort to save his own hide,&nbsp;
Pentangeli began cooperating with the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.040
government and was prepared to turn on Michael and&nbsp;
testify against him before a U.S. Senate panel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.680
He ultimately recanted his story and took his&nbsp;
own life to preserve the Sicilian code of honor.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.440
Like the first "Godfather" film, the sequel&nbsp;
yielded more than one Best Supporting Actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.920
nomination. This time, it pitted Gazzo&nbsp;
against legendary acting coach Lee Strasberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.840
who played Corleone family investor&nbsp;
Hyman Roth, as well as Robert De Niro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.400
who ended up winning for his portrayal of&nbsp;
a young Vito Corleone. Despite his loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.120
Gazzo's 1974 Oscar nom still gave him a&nbsp;
major career boost. He went on to appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.400
in the mob comedy "Cookie," the crime drama&nbsp;
"Fingers," and the 1977 thriller "Black Sunday."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.040
Gazzo also booked dozens of guest roles on&nbsp;
popular television shows, including "Taxi."

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.480
Before his acting career, Gazzo was&nbsp;
known as a respected playwright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.760
penning the 1955 classic "A Hatful of Rain,"&nbsp;
later turned into an Oscar-nominated film.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:47.868
Gazzo died on February 14, 1995, at the&nbsp;
age of 71, after suffering a stroke.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.240
Stage director Lee Strasberg has long been&nbsp;
celebrated in Hollywood for his work as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:57.520
legendary acting coach, both at his Actor's Studio&nbsp;
in New York City and at the Actor's Studio West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.760
in Los Angeles. He had a great number of famous&nbsp;
students including Marilyn Monroe, Paul Newman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.560
and James Dean, along with "Godfather" stars&nbsp;
Marlon Brando, Al Pacino and Robert De Niro.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:10.480
Strasberg's role as Corleone family&nbsp;
investor and business partner Hyman Roth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:15.040
who eventually betrays Michael Corleone, marked&nbsp;
a rare step away from his work as an educator.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:23.040
"And I said to myself - this&nbsp;
is the business we've chosen!"

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.680
It was only appropriate that the man who taught&nbsp;
so many legendary actors be nominated for an Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:32.240
himself, which came in the Best Supporting&nbsp;
Actor category. Strasberg was nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.920
opposite fellow "Godfather, Part II"&nbsp;
stars Michael V. Gazzo and Robert De&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:40.240
Niro. De Niro ended up winning the prize&nbsp;
for his portrayal of young Vito Corleone.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.960
Post "Godfather," Strasberg&nbsp;
popped up in a few more films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:46.960
notably the legal drama "And Justice&nbsp;
for All" opposite Pacino and the crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.080
comedy "Going in Style," with&nbsp;
George Burns and Art Carney.

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:55.280
But Strasberg's true passion was always teaching&nbsp;
– which he continued to do at the Actor's Studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.280 --> 00:12:01.801
where he remained until his death from a heart&nbsp;
attack on February 17, 1982. He was 80 years old.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.480
Even a powerful U.S. politician was no match&nbsp;
for Don Michael Corleone in "The Godfather:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.320
Part II," at least as G.D. Spradlin's&nbsp;
character, Senator Pat Geary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.320
painfully discovered. Spradlin perfectly&nbsp;
embodied a corrupt politician in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.480
playing a man who tried to extort a large sum of&nbsp;
money from Michael, including monthly personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.200
payouts, as the Corleone family tried&nbsp;
to expand its Las Vegas hotel operation.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:28.080
"I despise your masquerade. The&nbsp;
dishonest way you pose yourself."

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.440
Pressing Michael and the family&nbsp;
further with U.S. Senate hearings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.320
Geary eventually found himself&nbsp;
in a compromising position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.280
a strong signal that the Corleones are the only&nbsp;
people who can make offers you can't refuse.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:44.640
A lawyer-turned-oil-producer before finding his&nbsp;
calling in acting, Spradlin started off his career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:49.760
with a string of guest turns on such television&nbsp;
series as "I Spy" and "Bonanza." After his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:54.320
as the slimy Pat Geary in "The Godfather: Part&nbsp;
II," Spradlin enjoyed a long career in film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:59.120
Some of his features included "North Dallas Forty"&nbsp;
and "Ed Wood," with Bill Murray and Johnny Depp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:03.520
Notably, Spradlin worked with "The Godfather"&nbsp;
director Francis Ford Coppola a second time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.800
playing General Corman in the&nbsp;
acclaimed film "Apocalypse Now."

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.720
"Spradlin's final role came in 1999,&nbsp;
when he played legendary Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:14.800
Post executive editor Ben Bradlee in the&nbsp;
political comedy spoof "Dick," alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.760
Will Ferrell, Kirsten Dunst and Michelle Williams.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:22.240
"You're talking about the man who controls&nbsp;
access to the leader of the free world."

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:27.174
Spradlin died of natural causes&nbsp;
at age 90 on July 24, 2011.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:32.560
Legendary actor Eli Wallach didn't join "The&nbsp;
Godfather" saga until the third film in 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:37.440
bringing decades of movie and television&nbsp;
experience to the role of Don Osvaldo Altobello.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:42.720
The wily Altobello seems to be an ally of Michael&nbsp;
Corleone in the film, at least until the exposure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:47.520
of his betrayal, leading to his poisoning at the&nbsp;
opera house during the film's extended climax.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.627
"You're not telling me the whole truth, Vincenzo."

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.840
Wallach compiled an amazing 172 credits&nbsp;
over his 60-year career and was honored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:01.360
for his television work with five Primetime&nbsp;
Emmy Award nominations. The first in 1967&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:06.000
resulted in a win for Outstanding Supporting&nbsp;
Actor in a Drama for "The Poppy Is Also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.400
Flower," with the last nod coming in 2010&nbsp;
for Outstanding Guest Actor in a Comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.320
Series for "Nurse Jackie." While it took&nbsp;
six decades, Wallach was finally honored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:18.960
with the ultimate prize — an honorary&nbsp;
Oscar for Lifetime Achievement — in 2011.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:23.760
The actor's impressive film resume included&nbsp;
memorable turns in such features as the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.880
western "The Magnificent Seven" — where he starred&nbsp;
alongside Yul Brynner, Steve McQueen, and James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:34.000
Coburn — and director Sergio Leone's iconic "The&nbsp;
Good, the Bad, and the Ugly," playing opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:38.854
Clint Eastwood. He also appeared alongside&nbsp;
Marilyn Monroe in her last film, "The Misfits."

00:14:38.854 --> 00:14:44.240
"Well tell me. He doesn't know what&nbsp;
you're all about. He'll never know."

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.720
Wallach's last feature film role was in&nbsp;
the 2010 "Wall Street" sequel "Wall Street:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.434
Money Never Sleeps." The actor died on June&nbsp;
24, 2014, at age 98, of natural causes.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.800
Perhaps no "Godfather" movie death is more painful&nbsp;
to watch than that of Alex Rocco's Moe Greene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:05.600
the volatile, gravel-voiced Las Vegas&nbsp;
casino owner who roughs up Fredo Corleone.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:11.200
"Do you know who I am? I'm Moe Greene! I made my&nbsp;
bones when you were going out with cheerleaders!"

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:14.640
Greene's harsh treatment of Michael's&nbsp;
brother naturally gets to Michael. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.720
along with the fact that Greene is unwilling&nbsp;
to sell his casinos to the family, results in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:22.720
one of the most vivid assassinations&nbsp;
in the trilogy. Moe is shot through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:27.120
the eye while getting a massage during the&nbsp;
climactic bloodbath in the film's third act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:31.840
Greene's death is significant, as it is implied&nbsp;
in "The Godfather, Part II" that Hyman Rothis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:36.480
driven by the death of his childhood friend&nbsp;
and business partner to destroy Michael.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.067
"That kid's name was Moe Green."

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.840
With less than a dozen film and TV roles&nbsp;
to his credit, Rocco's career took off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:48.080
after his menacing portrayal of Greene.&nbsp;
Like his career before "The Godfather,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:52.880
the actor's work alternated between film&nbsp;
and television, but at a much busier pace.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:57.760
On television, Rocco guest-starred on such series&nbsp;
as "The Golden Girls" and "The Facts of Life."

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:01.120
"Wait, where did you ever get the&nbsp;
idea that we were getting married?"

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:04.800
Later, he had a role on "The George Carlin&nbsp;
Show, " playing George's best friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.560
the ex-con Harry Rossetti. Rocco won a primetime&nbsp;
Emmy in 1990 for Outstanding Supporting Actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.720
in a comedy series for his role as&nbsp;
Al Floss in "The Famous Teddy Z."

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:18.800
On film, Rocco appeared in "Cannonball&nbsp;
Run II," a seemingly popular destination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:22.560
for "Godfather" alumni, and played&nbsp;
gangster Jimmy Capp in "Get Shorty!",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:26.960
which made reference to his unforgettable death&nbsp;
in "The Godfather." He also appeared in "Dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:30.960
a Little Dream," "The Wedding Planner,"&nbsp;
and as the boisterous record executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.960
Sol Siler in Tom Hanks' directorial&nbsp;
debut, "That Thing You Do!"

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:38.000
"It is jammed - jammed with kids! And why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:42.720
Why? I'll tell ya why. These Wonders&nbsp;
are playing music like nectar!!"

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:48.427
Rocco died from pancreatic cancer&nbsp;
on July 18, 2015 at age 79.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.560
Virgil Sollozzo was a character living&nbsp;
on borrowed time in "The Godfather," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:57.387
Al Lettieri was the actor who gave Sollozzo&nbsp;
such an intense, menacing presence.

00:16:57.387 --> 00:16:59.280
"No more attempts on my father's life."

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:04.160
"What guarantees can I give&nbsp;
you Mike? I'm the hunted one!"

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:09.360
The arrival of Sollozzo, a heroin kingpin, marked&nbsp;
a sea change in the criminal underworld of New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:14.720
York City, where Don Vito Corleone had advised the&nbsp;
five crime families to rebuke the drug business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:20.000
Things came to a head when Sollozzo had a hand in&nbsp;
killing Vito's bodyguard Luca Brasi, and tried but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:24.934
failed to assassinate Vito, leading Michael to&nbsp;
commit his first murder on behalf of the family.

00:17:24.934 --> 00:17:26.080
"Do you know who I am?"

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:29.920
Prior to "The Godfather," Lettieri's&nbsp;
career was full of television appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.920
including guest roles on such classics&nbsp;
as "Perry Mason" and "Gunsmoke."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:38.640
Like many of his "Godfather" acting counterparts,&nbsp;
Lettieri's film opportunities expanded greatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:43.440
after his exposure in the film, landing him in&nbsp;
such features as "The Getaway" with Steve McQueen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:47.867
"McQ" opposite John Wayne, and "Mr.&nbsp;
Majestyk," starring Charles Bronson.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:52.800
Sadly, Lettieri's career was cut short when&nbsp;
he died of a heart attack on October 18,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:56.134
1975. He was just 47 years old.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:00.640
Michael Corleone passes the point of no return&nbsp;
in "The Godfather" when he assassinates family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:04.400
rival Virgil Sollozzo. But sitting right&nbsp;
there with him — and becoming Michael's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:09.467
second victim— is Captain Mark McCluskey,&nbsp;
played by Hollywood legend Sterling Hayden.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.840
"Stand him up! Stand him up straight!"

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:22.160
McCluskey is a corrupt police official&nbsp;
who doubles as an ad hoc bodyguard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:26.880
Sollozzo while also taking bribes from&nbsp;
the Tattaglia family. Michael proposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:32.320
to execute both Sollozzo and McCluskey after&nbsp;
a failed attempt on Don Vito Corleone's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:36.560
The public execution of the pair at a&nbsp;
public restaurant is brief, but brutal.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:38.880
"How's the Italian Food in this restaurant?"

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:43.440
Hayden was already a bona fide film star&nbsp;
by the time he was cast in "The Godfather,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:48.000
with more than four dozen roles to his credit,&nbsp;
including John Huston's "The Asphalt Jungle,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.720
and "Johnny Guitar." Hayden also appeared&nbsp;
in two acclaimed Stanley Kubrick films:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:57.600
"The Killing," and "Doctor Strangelove or: How&nbsp;
I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb."

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.120
"Total commitment"

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.800
The towering Hayden, who&nbsp;
stood at 6-feet 5-inches tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.160
relaxed his pace a bit after "The&nbsp;
Godfather." He still worked, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:11.920
starring in lighter fare like the&nbsp;
classic workplace comedy "9 to 5."

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:16.960
After a battle with cancer, Hayden&nbsp;
died on May 23, 1986. at the age of 70.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:21.120
Fans of "The Godfather" are quite&nbsp;
familiar with Carmela Corelone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:27.040
played by Morgana King. Also known as Mama&nbsp;
Corleone, Carmela is mother to Sonny, Fredo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:31.040
Michael, and Connie Corleone, as well&nbsp;
as the adoptive mother of Tom Hagen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:36.214
And even though she only has a few lines, it's&nbsp;
clear this is a woman who puts her family first.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:41.840
"You go see your children first. And then you&nbsp;
worry about waiting on line to see your brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.160
Like everybody else."

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:48.720
She's also the wife of Don Vito Corleone,&nbsp;
and suffers through the execution of Sonny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.120
and the death of Vito after his sudden heart&nbsp;
attack. On a more positive note, she will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:57.680
always be remembered for her singing at Connie's&nbsp;
wedding reception. This made sense, seeing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:02.800
how King was a professional singer who had an&nbsp;
impressive four-octave contralto vocal range.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:06.320
Aside from reprising her role as&nbsp;
Mama Corleone for "The Godfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:10.320
Part II," King only had a handful of&nbsp;
screen credits on both film and television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:15.600
most notably in a recurring role on the classic&nbsp;
daytime soap "All My Children" in 1993. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:21.520
last screen appearance came in 1997 in the Danny&nbsp;
Aiello crime drama, "A Brooklyn State of Mind."

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:27.200
King died from non-Hodgkin's lymphoma&nbsp;
on March 22, 2018 at the age of 87.

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:32.880
The death of Marlon Brando in 2004 was a loss&nbsp;
for fans of classic cinema and the "Godfather"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:37.680
series alike. A truly legendary presence,&nbsp;
Brando's storied film career began with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:42.000
star-making appearances in films like "On The&nbsp;
Waterfront" and "A Streetcar Named Desire."

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:49.427
"Hey Stella!"

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:55.360
What's interesting about Brando's turn as Don Vito&nbsp;
Corleone is that while he was already an iconic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.360 --> 00:21:00.960
Oscar-nominated actor, his career in Hollywood&nbsp;
was actually on a bit of a downslide. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:06.400
director Francis Ford Coppola had to fight to get&nbsp;
the actor cast after a string of box office bombs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:10.960
It finally happened, but only under strict&nbsp;
conditions set out by Paramount Pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:14.960
including a screen test — quite&nbsp;
unprecedented for such an esteemed actor.

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:20.080
The 47-year-old Brando's transformation into&nbsp;
the much older Don Vito was not lost on fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:23.200
critics and the film industry.&nbsp;
It earned him his second Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:27.200
Actor Oscar and revitalized his career.&nbsp;
Brando went to other acclaimed roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:31.920
including Kal-El's father, Jor-El, in director&nbsp;
Richard Donner's "Superman," and the crazed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:36.560
reclusive Colonel Kurtz in Coppola's&nbsp;
Vietnam War classic "Apocalypse Now."

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:40.800
Even though Brando famously spurned his&nbsp;
Best Actor Oscar for "The Godfather" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:44.720
protest the treatment of Native Americans,&nbsp;
the Motion Picture Academy rewarded him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:49.920
one last time with a Best Supporting Actor&nbsp;
nomination for the 1989 anti-apartheid drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:54.960
"A Dry White Season." Other notable roles&nbsp;
followed, including the 2005 comedy-drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:00.000
"Don Juan DeMarco" and the troubled 1996&nbsp;
remake of "The Island of Dr. Moreau."

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.667
"Look at yourself!"
 
"I understand that I must be shocking to you."

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:09.200
Brando's final feature film appearance came&nbsp;
opposite Robert De Niro and Edward Norton in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:15.440
2001 heist thriller "The Score." The legendary&nbsp;
actor died three years later on July 1, 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.800
at age 80 from pulmonary fibrosis — leaving behind&nbsp;
one of the greatest legacies in film history.

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:25.440
Fans of "The Godfather" trilogy know&nbsp;
that Sonny Corleone — played by James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:30.800
Caan in the first two films — had a legendary&nbsp;
reputation among all of the mafia families as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:34.134
heir to the Corleone empire — and&nbsp;
as a guy with a serious temper.

00:22:34.243 --> 00:22:37.020
"Hey come on knock it off!"

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:37.520
"Sonny!"

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:38.240
"Punk."

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:43.200
Furthering their war with the other Five Families,&nbsp;
Sonny gets on the last nerve of Emilio Barzini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:48.560
who sets a trap for the hot-headed Corleone heir.&nbsp;
Sonny is brutally killed on the causeway, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:52.960
his brother Michael to finish the war he started&nbsp;
and turn the Corleones into the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:57.360
crime family ever known. At the end of "Part&nbsp;
III," Sonny's illegitimate son Vincent, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:02.533
by Andy Garcia, takes over the empire, something&nbsp;
which certainly would've made his father proud.

00:23:02.533 --> 00:23:07.120
“What did he say? Bada beep bada bap bada boop. He&nbsp;
wants us to send Michael to hear the proposition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:10.080
And the promise is that the deal&nbsp;
is so good that we can't refuse."

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:15.040
Although Sonny's life was cut short,&nbsp;
actor James Caan had an incredibly long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:19.360
and successful career spanning nearly 60&nbsp;
years, with hits in almost every decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:23.440
Though his most recognizable work might&nbsp;
be as Sonny, Caan also had starring roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.280
in many other major films, including&nbsp;
Michael Maan's "Thief," Rob Reiner's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:32.240
"Misery," Francis Ford Coppola's "Gardens of&nbsp;
Stone," and Wes Anderson's "Bottle Rocket."

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:35.040
"I'm-a tell you something kid -&nbsp;
you got the guts of a damn lion."

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:40.320
But perhaps Caan's most famous performance outside&nbsp;
of "The Godfather" is also the most unlikely one:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:45.280
his comedic turn as Will Ferrell's father in&nbsp;
"Elf." Cann's portrayal of children's book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:50.560
publisher Walter Hobbes in Jon Favreau's Christmas&nbsp;
classic brought him a whole new legion of fans.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:55.703
Sadly, the actor died on July 6, 2022, and&nbsp;
the film industry would never be the same.

